{"findings":[{"problem":"The confirm_element description still says \"Only a person confirms.\", and the Element object description still reads \"proposed by an agent or confirmed by a person\". The new confirm_wording_element lets an agent confirm, so these statements are now false or at least inconsistent.","where":"SKILL.md, Authoring actions: confirm_element; Authoring objects: Element description","kind":"contradiction"},{"problem":"The agent's confirmation goes through the same kernel service `confirm_element` with approver = actor.id. That service is built around a person approving, so it may refuse an agent approver, or it may record the agent as the approver of a confirmed element. The change does not say how the kernel treats a non-human approver, and nothing marks the confirmation as agent-made. The confirmed element looks the same as one a person confirmed.","where":"SKILL.md, confirm_wording_element effect (kernel service confirm_element, approver = actor.id)","kind":"behaviour"},{"problem":"The only guard on agent confirmation is the wording_only flag, which the proposer sets (optional boolean on propose_element) and nothing checks against the element's content. Any substantive element proposed with wording_only true can be confirmed with no person involved. This bypasses the human-confirmation control, and the preconditions do not stop it. No action lets a person review or clear the flag later.","where":"SKILL.md, propose_element (wording_only param) and confirm_wording_element precondition `wording-only`","kind":"other"},{"problem":"The transition table lets confirm_wording_element move an `unknown` element straight to `confirmed`. An unknown element is one deliberately marked as not known yet. Letting an agent confirm it, without a person, undermines the \"not known yet, instead of guessing\" meaning.","where":"SKILL.md, Element.review transitions: unknown / confirm_wording_element","kind":"behaviour"},{"problem":"The new `agent` role is added to the roles list and to the authoring actors, but only confirm_wording_element uses it. There is no world identity for the agent, only \"an automated agent\", unlike owner (ana) and expert (eli). The scenario's `Role agent` therefore has no fixture actor to run as. The Human-only list and the \"Actors\" lines were not otherwise reconciled.","where":"SKILL.md, Authoring Roles; EXAMPLES.md, an-agent-confirms-only-wording-fixes","kind":"reference"}]}
```

